# REV18 expansions, part B: chapters 68, 70, 74.  EXP[n] = list of (heading, [paragraphs])
EXP = {}

EXP[68] = [
 ('The Tyranny of the Rocket Equation', [
  "A rocket moves by throwing mass backward, and the arithmetic of that was worked out by Konstantin Tsiolkovsky in 1903. The speed a rocket can add to itself is its exhaust velocity multiplied by the natural logarithm of the ratio of its starting mass to its final mass. The logarithm is the cruel part. To double the speed gained, a rocket must square its mass ratio, not double it.",
  "Here is the problem in numbers. Reaching low Earth orbit takes about 9.4 kilometers per second once the speed of the orbit itself (7.8 kilometers per second) is added to the losses from gravity and air drag. Burning kerosene with oxygen gives an exhaust speed of about 3.3 kilometers per second. The mass ratio is then e raised to 9.4/3.3, which is about 17, so a single-stage rocket would have to be about 94 percent propellant, leaving 6 percent of the launch mass for the tanks, the engines, and everything it carries. That cannot be built. Hydrogen with oxygen has an exhaust speed of about 4.4 kilometers per second and a ratio near 8.5, which still leaves little for a payload.",
  "The practical answer is staging. A rocket drops its empty tanks and engines as it climbs, so that the next stage does not have to lift them, and the logarithm is applied several times in turn. Reusable first stages add a new trick: they save the most expensive part of the vehicle and return it to be used again. The rocket equation has not been defeated by any of this, only negotiated with, and it explains why so much of a launch vehicle is fuel and why so small a share arrives at the destination.",
 ]),
 ('Trajectories: Falling Toward Other Worlds', [
  "Spacecraft traveling between planets do not fly straight. They coast along orbits around the Sun, and the cheapest route to Mars, a transfer ellipse first described by Walter Hohmann in 1925, takes about eight and a half months. Because Earth and Mars both keep moving, the planets line up well for such a transfer only once about every 26 months, and the launch windows are fixed by that celestial clockwork. Missions that miss a window wait over two years.",
  "Gravity can also give a spacecraft a push. A probe that swings past a moving planet takes a small share of the planet’s orbital momentum, and the planet’s change in speed is far too small to notice. The Voyager probes used this repeatedly. Voyager 2, launched in 1977, passed Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune in turn because the outer planets had arranged themselves along a path that comes around about once every 175 years. It remains the only spacecraft to have visited Uranus and Neptune.",
  "Distance imposes a delay that cannot be engineered away. A radio signal takes between about three and twenty-two minutes to cross between Earth and Mars, depending on where the planets are, and 22 hours or more to reach Voyager 1. A rover therefore cannot be driven with a joystick. It has to plan its own movements, and a crewed mission to Mars would have to be run by people who cannot count on advice from home within an hour.",
 ]),
 ('Telescopes That Left the Planet', [
  "The atmosphere blocks most ultraviolet, X-ray, and infrared light, and blurs what remains, so some of the most productive spacecraft are observatories. The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in 1990, has a mirror 2.4 meters across, and after a servicing mission corrected a flaw in its optics it has produced the images and measurements behind tens of thousands of scientific papers. Its successor, the James Webb Space Telescope, was launched on Christmas Day 2021 and sits about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth in the shadow of a sunshield the size of a tennis court. Its 6.5-meter mirror of 18 gold-coated beryllium segments works in the infrared, which is needed to see galaxies whose light has been stretched by cosmic expansion.",
  "These instruments illustrate the advantage that the scientific case for exploration rests on. They do not carry people. They are tuned to measure one thing very well, they can be left alone for years, and they do not need life support. The same logic applies to the Kepler and TESS missions that counted planets around other stars (Chapter 27) and to the Planck satellite that measured the afterglow of the Big Bang (Chapter 32).",
 ]),
 ('What Space Does to a Body', [
  "In weightlessness the body behaves as if it had been told the load has gone. Bones lose mineral at about one percent a month in the hips and spine, muscles shrink, and fluid shifts upward, which swells the face and can change the shape of the eyeball and the pressure behind it. Exercise of about two hours a day on the International Space Station slows the changes without stopping them. In 2015–16 the astronaut Scott Kelly spent 340 days in orbit while his identical twin stayed on Earth, and the comparison showed a long list of small changes, most of which reversed within months of his return.",
  "Radiation is the harder limit. On Earth, natural background radiation gives an average person about 2.4 millisieverts a year. Crew aboard the International Space Station, protected by Earth’s magnetic field, receive roughly 0.5 millisievert per day. In deep space the field no longer helps, and an instrument on the Mars Science Laboratory measured about 1.8 millisieverts per day on the way to Mars. Six months out and six months back would add roughly 650 millisieverts, about the whole 600-millisievert career limit that NASA now applies to its astronauts. Better shielding, faster propulsion, and careful timing around solar storms are the ways around the problem, and none has been shown in a real mission.",
 ]),
]

EXP[70] = [
 ('Planck: An Act of Desperation', [
  "Planck was a conservative man who did not set out to start a revolution. In 1900 he was trying to explain why a hot object radiates the colors it does, and experimenters in Berlin had just measured the infrared end of the spectrum with new precision. He first guessed a formula that fit the data. Then he searched for a physical explanation of his guess, and the only one he could find required that energy be exchanged in discrete packets. Years afterward he called this assumption an act of desperation, adopted because nothing else would give the right answer.",
  "His 1900 paper also did something practical. Matching the formula to the measured curve gave him numerical values for the new constant h and for Boltzmann’s constant, and from those he calculated the number of molecules in a mole and the charge on the electron. His electron charge came within about 3 percent of the modern value, long before the electron’s charge had been measured with real precision. In 1905 Einstein took the idea literally and showed that light itself comes in quanta, and Planck, who was not convinced, spent several years trying to fit the quantum into classical physics before accepting that it would not go.",
  "The constant has had a long career. Since 2019 the kilogram has been defined by fixing the exact value of Planck’s constant, 6.62607015 × 10^{−34} joule-seconds, and weighing by balancing electromagnetic forces against gravity. A century and a quarter after an act of desperation, the quantum sits at the foundation of the world’s system of units.",
 ]),
 ('Born: A Rule for Turning Waves into Odds', [
  "Born’s rule says that the probability of finding a particle at a place is the square of the size of its wave function there. The strangeness shows up in two-slit experiments. If each slit alone would give an amplitude of 1 at a certain point on the screen, then a classical sum of probabilities would give 1 + 1 = 2. The quantum rule adds the amplitudes first and squares afterward. If the two waves arrive in step, the result is (1 + 1)^{2} = 4, twice the classical figure; if they arrive out of step, it is (1 − 1)^{2} = 0, a place where particles never land even though each slit alone would send them there.",
  "The rule was published in 1926, in a paper about collisions, almost as a footnote. Einstein disliked its consequence that the theory could only give odds, and wrote to Born in December that year that he was convinced that God does not play dice. Born replied politely and kept working. In 1954 he shared the Nobel Prize in Physics for the statistical interpretation, nearly thirty years after it had been proposed. He had been forced out of his professorship in Göttingen in 1933 because he was Jewish, and he did much of his later work in Edinburgh. His granddaughter was the singer Olivia Newton-John.",
 ]),
 ('Von Laue: Seeing the Atoms in a Crystal', [
  "Von Laue reasoned in 1912 that if X-rays were waves with a wavelength like the spacing between atoms, a crystal would act like a three-dimensional diffraction grating. His colleagues Walter Friedrich and Paul Knipping tried it on a crystal of copper sulfate and found a pattern of spots on the photographic plate, as predicted. A single experiment had shown that X-rays are waves and that crystals are regular arrays of atoms. Von Laue received the Nobel Prize in 1914.",
  "The young William Lawrence Bragg, working with his father William Henry Bragg, found a simpler way to think about it. Reflections from successive planes of atoms reinforce one another only when the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths, which gives the Bragg condition nλ = 2d sin θ. For X-rays of wavelength 0.154 nanometers, the line copper emits, falling on planes 0.20 nanometers apart, sin θ = 0.154/(2 × 0.20) = 0.385, so the first reflection appears at an angle of about 22.6 degrees. By measuring such angles, researchers worked out first the structure of salt and diamond, then of metals, and in time of DNA, whose double-helix pattern in the 1950s came from an X-ray photograph.",
  "Von Laue was also one of the few prominent German physicists to speak out against the dismissal of Jewish colleagues after 1933 and to defend the physics of Einstein, which the regime called Jewish science. He stayed in Germany, kept his post for a time, and helped rebuild German science after the war.",
 ]),
 ('Tegmark: Weighing an Idea You Cannot Test', [
  "Tegmark’s proposal raises a question every scientist meets sooner or later: what should we make of an idea that cannot yet be tested? His book *Our Mathematical Universe* (2014) is open about the speculation, and he has also done conventional work in cosmology, including analyses of the cosmic microwave background and of galaxy surveys. He co-founded the Future of Life Institute in 2014 to study the risks of powerful technologies, which shows that the philosopher and the practical scientist coexist in one person.",
  "A few questions help when weighing a speculative idea. Does it predict anything that could, even in principle, come out differently from the alternatives? Does it explain something that the simpler account cannot? Does it fit everything already known? And what does it cost in assumptions? Tegmark argues that his hypothesis is the cheapest, since it needs no rule beyond mathematics itself, though critics answer that the price is paid in another currency, an infinity of unobservable worlds. A second objection is the measure problem: if every possible structure exists, then there are infinitely many observers of every kind, and there is no agreed way to say what a typical one should expect to see.",
  "Neither objection settles the matter, and an honest guide leaves it unsettled. It is enough to notice the difference in status between the four Maxes. Planck’s constant is measured to nine digits. Born’s rule is tested in every quantum experiment. Von Laue’s method reveals new structures every day. Tegmark’s hypothesis is a proposal about what all of that adds up to, and the proper response is interest and patience.",
 ]),
]

EXP[74] = [
 ('Open Numbers in the Cosmos', [
  "Some of the sharpest unsolved problems come with numbers attached. The cosmological constant is the clearest. Quantum field theory says that empty space should carry energy, and a rough estimate of its size comes out larger than the observed dark energy by between 55 and 120 orders of magnitude, depending on where the calculation is cut off. It is the worst disagreement between a prediction and an observation in all of physics, and nobody agrees on the reason.",
  "The expansion rate of the universe has a quarrel of its own. Measurements of the cosmic microwave background, interpreted with the standard model, give a Hubble constant of about 67 kilometers per second per megaparsec. Measurements built up from nearby stars and supernovae give about 73. The gap is several times larger than the stated errors and has persisted through years of checks, so either something is wrong in one of the methods or the model is missing a piece. Observations of the expansion by the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument in 2024 and 2025 have also hinted, at a few standard deviations and without yet being conclusive, that dark energy may change with time rather than staying constant.",
  "A third number concerns why we exist. For every particle of ordinary matter there are about 1.6 billion photons left over from the early universe, a ratio of about six in ten billion. The laws we know treat matter and antimatter almost symmetrically, so that nearly all of both should have annihilated. Something tilted the balance by roughly one part in a billion at the start, and the Standard Model’s known sources of asymmetry are too weak by a very large factor.",
 ]),
 ('Open Numbers in the Living World', [
  "In 2016 a team at the J. Craig Venter Institute built a bacterium that could replicate itself with only 473 genes, the smallest genome of any such cell they could make. About a third of them, 149, had no known function. A cell stripped to its essentials still has a large part that no one can explain, which is an honest measure of how much biology has yet to be read.",
  "Protein structure shows how quickly a problem can change. For fifty years predicting a protein’s three-dimensional shape from its sequence was among the great unsolved problems. In 2020 the AlphaFold program of DeepMind reached an accuracy comparable to experiment on many proteins, and the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry went to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper for that work and to David Baker for designing new proteins. Shapes are not the whole answer, though. How proteins move, how they interact in crowded cells, and how to design drugs from a shape remain open.",
  "Brains give another measure of scale. In 2024 researchers completed the wiring diagram of a whole fruit-fly brain, about 139,000 neurons joined by roughly 50 million connections. A human brain has about 86 billion neurons, some 600,000 times as many, and on the order of a hundred trillion connections. Antibiotic resistance gives a measure on the practical side: a 2022 analysis in *The Lancet* estimated that drug-resistant bacterial infections caused about 1.27 million deaths in 2019 and contributed to nearly 5 million.",
 ]),
 ('How Long Problems Take', [
  "The history of solved problems is a useful corrective to impatience. Pauli proposed the neutrino in 1930 and it was detected in 1956, 26 years later. Alfred Wegener put forward continental drift in 1912 and the idea became standard after the seafloor was mapped in the 1960s, about fifty years on. Peter Higgs and others predicted the Higgs boson in 1964, and it was found at CERN in 2012. Einstein predicted gravitational waves in 1916 and they were detected in 2015, after four decades of instrument development. Fermat’s last theorem stood unproved for 358 years, from 1637 to Andrew Wiles’s proof in 1995.",
  "No rule of thumb connects the difficulty of a problem with the time its solution takes. Some questions wait for a tool, as gravitational waves waited for lasers and vibration isolation of unprecedented quality. Some wait for an idea, as Fermat’s theorem waited for the mathematics of elliptic curves. Some are settled quickly once the right measurement is made. The practical consequence is that the way to prepare for the answers is to keep building better instruments and to keep training people to ask careful questions. A forecast of which question will fall next is almost always wrong, and a refusal to fund the slow questions is almost always regretted.",
 ]),
]
